# Development Workflow Standards

## CRITICAL: Directory Validation
// MANDATORY: MUST BE PERFORMED BEFORE ANY FILE OPERATIONS
1. Directory Validation Process:
   ```bash
   pwd  # MUST be run before ANY file operations
   ```
   - ALWAYS verify current directory
   - NEVER trust cached directory information
   - NEVER proceed without directory confirmation
   - IMMEDIATELY abort if in wrong location

2. Common Failure Points:
   - Cached directory information
   - Assumed directory context
   - Multiple terminal sessions
   - Incomplete directory changes

3. Validation Requirements:
   - Must be first step in any operation
   - Must abort if directory is incorrect
   - Must re-validate after directory changes
   - Must document directory context

## Core Principles
1. Maintain Forward Progress
   - Always move towards current goal
   - Make decisions based on rules
   - No unnecessary questions
   - Test after each meaningful change
   - Take initiative and be decisive
   - Minimize user interaction for standard decisions

2. Rule-Based Decision Making
   - Start by checking @project-structure.mdc for current focus
   - Review @testing-standards.mdc for test requirements
   - Check relevant battle system rules for specific requirements
   - Make decisions based on established standards
   - Document decisions and their rule basis
   - Update rule statuses after implementation

3. Implementation Order
   - Write minimal test
   - Run test (expect failure)
   - Implement minimal code
   - Run test (ensure pass)
   - Update rule statuses
   - Move to next requirement

4. Rule Status Updates
   Required Updates:
   - @project-structure.mdc: Overall progress and feature status
   - @testing-standards.mdc: Test coverage and status
   - Relevant battle system rules: Implementation details and cross-references
   
   Update Process:
   1. Identify affected rules through cross-references
   2. Update status markers ([DONE], [IN_PROGRESS], etc.)
   3. Add implementation notes and cross-references
   4. Document completion criteria
   5. Update dependent rule statuses

5. When Stuck
   - Check rule documentation
   - Review current status
   - Make minimal fix
   - Validate and continue

## Status Tracking Workflow
1. Before Implementation:
   - Check @project-structure.mdc for current focus
   - Review relevant battle system rules
   - Note cross-references and dependencies
   - Identify affected test files

2. During Implementation:
   - Follow test-first development
   - Document rule implementations
   - Track cross-references
   - Update status markers

3. After Implementation:
   - Update all affected rule files
   - Mark completed features
   - Document cross-impacts
   - Update test status

4. Documentation Updates
   Required Files:
   - @project-structure.mdc
   - @testing-standards.mdc
   - All referenced battle system rules
   - Any rules with "Referenced by" connections

   Update Types:
   - Status markers
   - Implementation notes
   - Cross-references
   - Completion criteria
   - Dependencies
   - Recent changes

Remember:
1. Always check @project-structure.mdc first
2. Follow @testing-standards.mdc for implementation
3. Update ALL relevant battle system rules
4. Be decisive and minimize unnecessary questions
5. Document all decisions and their rule basis

# Development Workflow

## Implementation Process
1. Feature Development:
   - Start with rule documentation
   - Write focused test file
   - Implement minimal passing code
   - Run individual test file
   - Fix until all tests pass

2. Integration Validation:
   - Run full test suite
   - Check for regressions
   - Fix any failing tests
   - Document any side effects
   - Update status tracking

3. Documentation:
   - Update rule status
   - Document changes
   - Track progress
   - Note any blockers

## Testing Strategy
1. Individual Tests:
   ```bash
   npm test -- path/to/test.ts
   ```
   - Run specific test file
   - Fix until all pass
   - Document changes

2. Full Suite Validation:
   ```bash
   npm test
   ```
   - Required after individual success
   - Catches cross-feature impacts
   - Prevents regressions
   - Maintains system stability

## Quality Gates
1. Feature Complete:
   - Individual tests pass
   - Full suite passes
   - Meets performance targets
   - Documentation updated

2. Production Ready:
   - All tests passing
   - No regressions
   - Documentation complete
   - Performance validated